                  "question": "When is it MOST appropriate to use foam rolling?",
                  "options": [
                    {"text":"As your primary daily mobility practice","correct":false},
                    {"text":"After active work, as the main course of recovery","correct":false},
                    {"text":"As a brief warmup before active end-range training","correct":true},
                    {"text":"Anytime tightness shows up, for as long as it takes","correct":false}
                  ],
                  "explanation": "Foam rolling has a legitimate role as a brief adjunct — a 60-second neural primer before active mobility so you can access a slightly bigger range during CARs and loaded end-range work. It is not the foundation, not a daily standalone practice, and not what builds lasting change. Active work changes the joint structurally and neurologically. Passive work just turns down the volume temporarily."
